From e0380455271a9a3c90af160192da34e52a9e8679 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 01:36:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] sessions: a call-cache shard is synced before its rename, and
 a failed rename is said (#2814)

What changed

writeCalls' non-append path no longer builds a temp file by hand with
os.CreateTemp -> Write -> Close -> _ = os.Rename. The frame it already has
in memory is written with steady.WriteFile (internal/sessions/call_cache.go),
which flushes the temp file to the disk before renaming it in and returns
the rename's error. A failed write leaves the previous shard as it was, and
the error is logged once per shard path ("<path> wasn't written over: ...")
until a write succeeds; pruneCalls forgets the paths of sessions that are
gone. The abandoned-temp sweep in pruneCalls also recognises
steady.WriteFile's ".<shard>.*" temps beside the older calls-*.tmp ones.

The append path is unchanged: it still opens the shard O_APPEND and writes
the frame (whose header carries its own CRC32) in place, and a torn tail is
caught by readCalls as before.

Why

A shard renamed in before its data reached the disk can come back all zero
or cut short after a forced restart; readCalls catches it, but every such
shard is a full re-parse of the session file at the next start, all at
once. A rename that fails (on Windows a reader or a virus scanner holding
the shard open) was silent, so the cache never landed and nothing said so,
and every read re-parsed the whole session file until a rename happened to
succeed. steady.WriteFile already existed for exactly this after #1505 and
is used by logins.go, plugin/store.go, mcpauth, kiro, codex_autoreset and
lastgood.

Run (darwin/arm64, Go 1.26.8; base upstream/main 95ef45fda4b3)

go test -tags nogui -count=1 ./internal/sessions/          ok  11.365s (529 tests)
go test -tags nogui -count=1 ./internal/steady/           ok   1.060s (2 tests)
go test -race -tags nogui -count=1 ./internal/sessions/   ok  24.117s
gofmt -l internal/sessions/ internal/steady/              empty
go vet -tags nogui ./internal/sessions/ ./internal/steady/ ok
GOOS=linux go vet -tags nogui ./internal/sessions/        ok
GOOS=windows go vet -tags nogui ./internal/sessions/      ok
go build -tags nogui ./...                                ok
GOOS=linux go build -tags nogui ./...                     ok
GOOS=windows go build -tags nogui -o /dev/null .          ok

TestCallShardIsFlushedBeforeItsRenameAndAFailedRenameIsSaid is RED on base
("a shard whose flush failed was renamed in (<nil>)") and GREEN with the
change; dropping the flush, or swallowing the rename's error again, makes
it RED again. One race run of the whole package failed once in nine on a
pre-existing race between testenv.Zone and a background saveCache goroutine
in TestOpenCodeSubagents; the same failure reproduces on a clean
upstream/main copy (1 in 3 full race runs) and is untouched by this change.

